<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Shimano 600 EX Arabesque: Japanese precision, mechanical innovation and decorative patterns in one groupset."/><title>Shimano 600 EX Arabesque · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="components" danishPath="/komponenter/shimano-600-ex-arabesque/"/><main><article class="component-page">
<header class="component-hero"><div><p class="section-label">Component archive · 1978</p><h1>Shimano 600 EX<br/><em>Arabesque</em></h1></div><p>When Shimano turned mechanics into decoration.</p></header><figure class="component-hero-image"><img src={`${root}/group-illustration.webp`} alt="Historical illustration of the Shimano 600 EX Arabesque groupset"/><figcaption>The complete 600 EX series. Historical catalogue illustration.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="component-opening"><p class="section-label">Form and function</p><div><p>The curling patterns on derailleur, crank and levers almost belong on an Italian hand-built frame. That was the point.</p></div></section><section class="component-facts" aria-label="Shimano 600 EX Arabesque facts">{facts.map(([l,v])=><div><dt>{l}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>)}</section>
<section class="component-narrative"><aside><span>Japan</span><strong>1978</strong><i></i><span>Europe</span></aside><div class="component-copy"><p class="dropcap">Shimano launched 600 EX Arabesque in 1978 while establishing itself in a European market where Campagnolo remained strong on finer racing bicycles. The group had to work well and look beautiful.</p><p>Shimano’s product history says its patterns were designed to harmonise with the craft of elegant chromoly frames.</p><blockquote>Japanese precision dressed for the European racing bicycle.</blockquote><h2>A small mechanical trick</h2><p>The rear derailleur’s <em>Hatch-Plate Mechanism</em> allowed the chain and derailleur to be removed without first splitting the chain or removing a pulley bolt.</p><p>A cassette Freehub offered more sprocket combinations; period catalogues show five- and six-speed versions.</p></div></section>
<section class="catalog-spread"><figure><img src={`${root}/catalog-drivetrain.webp`} alt="Catalogue page showing the drivetrain" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>RD-6200, SL-6200/6210, FD-6200 and FC-6200/6210.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${root}/catalog-brakes-freehub.webp`} alt="Catalogue page showing Freehub and brakes" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Freehub, brakes and headset from the series.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="friction-section"><div><p class="section-label">Feel at the fingertips</p><h2>The rider found the gear</h2></div><div><p>There were no clicks or automatic indexing. The rider moved the down-tube lever until the chain ran cleanly.</p><p>It required practice but gave a direct connection between hand, cable, derailleur and chain. SIS indexing came later in the 600 series.</p></div></section>
<section class="component-closing"><p class="section-label">Legacy</p><blockquote>Modern components are lighter, faster and more precise — but are they more beautiful?</blockquote><p>Arabesque became unusual because its decoration did not conceal the mechanics; it drew attention to them.</p></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><p>Dates, purpose and technical details were checked against Shimano’s product history, service instructions and the catalogue material shown.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.shimano.com/en/100th/history/products/17.html">Shimano: 100 Products History — Shimano 600</a></li><li><a href="https://si.shimano.com/en/pdfs/si/N-92/SI-N-92-000-00-ENG.pdf">Shimano: 600 EX Freehub service instruction</a></li></ul></div></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/components/`}>← All components</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1970-1979/`}>Read about 1970–1979 →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
